From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: Free Will

John K Clark said in X-Message-Number: #93-10-821:-
>( Please note I said I was "absolutely sure" I did not
>say I was correct, a very different thing. A person can be
>absolutely positively 100% certain of something and still be
>dead wrong, in fact it's very common.) 

Sounds like a Russell paradox.  If someone believes the second sentence is
true, then they cannot be 100% "absolutely sure" of anything; they must
therefore doubt the statement's truth.  And in this case the "absolutely
sure" of sentence one also becomes meaningless.  On the other hand, anyone
who is absolutely sure of anything would reject the statement as false and
it would not be incongruent for them to believe in its falsehood.

>Even if the world were totally deterministic (and we know it
>isn't) we would still have a feeling of choice and we could
>still be FREE to do what we want to do ; it's just that "what we
>want to do" would be caused by something else.
	
Here's one way I like to look at it:  For aeons life has been enslaved
by genetic and environmental determinism; the creatures were simply
puppets with their course mapped out and planned.  Well, we're here now,
and Nature had better watch out, as it's our turn to start doing the
"causing", shaping the world and the stars to our own requirements.  The
former puppets are knocking at autonomy's door.  If Nature had known what
a Pandora's Box she was opening, she might have had second thoughts.
	
>	   >We can also choose to make random arational decisions if we
>	   >want, and this is "free will".
	   
>Could be true but it's not very useful because it doesn't really
>answer anything, it just kicks the problem upstairs. How do we
>choose to make random arational decisions? It's like religious
>people who say "God must exist because something must have made
>the Universe" but they never think to ask the question "Who made
>God?".
	
Let's say I plan to travel to some place and I judge that there are two
routes of equal value.  I've eliminated the others as clearly being
inferior, but the choice between these two is an arational choice.  So
I mark one as "even", one as "odd".  I pick a number roughly halfway
between one and ten, giving it six decimal places, raise e to that power, 
and look at the sixth significant digit.  This tells me which route to
take, as already marked out.  So there can be no meaningful correlation
between the decision and any previous events (unless I was somehow able
to unconsciously do the calculation in advance, unaided).
   
>		>purpose and order can arise spontaneously.  It doesn't have
>		>to be created, as Darwin showed us.  
		
>When talking about purpose you have to ask " purpose for who?".

Order can spontaneously arise from randomness, then we take that order
and use it for our own purposes, whatever purposes we choose.  And
the choice is freely made by ourselves -- our memecomplexes.

>		>Electricity is involved in the brain, but I think the
>		>difference is that having electrodes                    
>		>connected up whilst undergoing brain surgery is not 'natural'
		
>Neither is talking on the telephone, using a computer, watching
>TV or reading a book yet these things change our thoughts,
>desires and behavior. If ,like the electrodes, they just give us
>a "illusion" of free will, how can we tell if were ever really
>free? I don't much like natural things myself, after all nothing
>is more natural than death.
	
I did go on to point out that this was a particular case of intervention;
the brain surgery was not even intended to give an accurate perception.  
I'd certainly be willing to have my natural parts augmented or replaced
once we can do this with no serious (or even subtle but crucial) side
effects.  But until these facilities are available, our survival skills
are sharpest and our perception most accurate without having electrodes
or drugs forced into the brain, and anyone standing on top of a cliff or
a window ledge would do well to remember this.
	
>                         imagine a parallel world in which the
>people use the same English words we do but they mean different
>things, sky means dog, beauty means potato etc. In one such
>world this post would mean a vigorous defense of socialism in
>another it would mean hard core pornography and in another it
>would mean the operating instructions for a new type of can
>opener.

The particular parallel worlds of interest to us are a group which are
all extremely alike.  When the differences are very slight there is no
problem mapping the space and time of each one directly to that of its
counterparts, so that objects are effectively superposed at the same
points in space and time.  And if the objects are brains, this is
exactly how it will appear to them.  But once the universes become too
dissimilar then the common frames of reference disappear and it becomes
meaningless and non-operational to attempt to say which point in one is
the equivalent in another.  There might be some versions of reality
where this post is the assembly instructions for a new type of mousetrap,
but to permit such a reality would probably require so many drastic
changes that the space on space correlation is totally lost.  Such points
in reality could be considered to be well outside of our mind cone; they
would be termed "absolute elsewhere" or reality-spacelike-separated.

>Hans Moravec calls these ideas "Universal Realization" and is
>currently writing a book about it. His main points:
>		
>1) Anything can simulate anything.

This has to be wrong, unless it's agreed that the simulation can be as 
different from the real thing as chalk and cheese.  "We're about to run
a simulation of Albert Einstein using this blade of grass and a wet
blanket."  In *Extropy* #9 David Ross agrees with Searle's point that
you can't simulate a flame on a computer and produce the flame's heat.
This isn't to say that you can't simulate a mind on a computer.  But
there must be some possible architectures where the inaccuracies of the
simulations are so subtle that they take a long time to show up, with a
potential for some massive lawsuits.  "I was uploaded into this machine
736 years ago, and it's now become apparent to me that my free will was
an illusion until 12 years ago when I upgraded some modules / an essential
element of my identity was lost during the transfer.  That's why I lost
9,627 TeraCredits on that scam / crashed my asteroid onto Mars."

>                                Moravec goes as far as to
>say that a interpretation must exist that can find a mind even
>in a rock. The reason we can't detect them is that were too stupid.

I think it's more likely that they don't exist.  I used to have some
ideas on this involving benevolent highly advanced silicon-based aliens,
technologies capable of scanning brains to transfer the identity over to
an arrangement of quartz crystals, the building of stone circles such as
Stonehenge, "reincarnation" (although pretty useless due to grossly
distorted and almost impossible to retrieve identity) and maybe even
"ghosts".  The theory had so many holes (like a requirement for something
like telepathy and maybe telekinesis) that I prefer to quietly forget it
(better to concentrate on more operational topics!) 

If we were living in a single deterministic universe, then the anti-free
will side would have won; if our perception and self-knowledge could
always be relied upon to be totally accurate the free will side would
have triumphed.  But we've been dealt all these ingredients to play with:
Lamarckian memetic evolution, randomness, indeterminism, determinism,
parallel worlds, downward causation, highly complex protoplasm structures
that are the result of four billion years' biological evolution....  The
only thing that really wants scrapping is Darwinian biological evolution
-- it's cruel, is now outclassed, and has simply outlived its usefulness.  
Rather than dividing into "rational" and "irrational" we should be trying
to integrate each part into a grand unified theory that allows us to take
our rightful place in the whole scheme of things.

There's so many loopholes that it's easy to believe that free will is
more than a mere "illusion", so avoiding the "mechanical philosophy" and
its consequences as described in Morris Berman's piece in Bits and Bytes. 
I wouldn't be able to believe in "God" or "Santa Claus" even if I wanted
to, but I can choose to believe in Santa Cruz's Klaus! and in free will.

