From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: NEWS/UK: How computers will be smarter than humans

Paul Cisek comments on Marvin Minsky's recent opinions on AI:

> This is an explanation?  That lots of these little "mind"less homunculi work
> together to build a "mind"?  And this is supposed to be enough of a formal
> theory to go and build intelligent machines? Has anyone ever actually _done_
> this?  (And I don't mean written in up in a science fiction story).
> 
> I'm sorry to post such flames to this list, but I'm continously amazed at how
> silly this guy has become.  It's an unfortunate trend in science that the
> giants of the past often feel justified in their later years to loosen their
> formal thinking and speculate in a vacuum to eager reporters.  And it's also
> unfortunate how eager laymen get fooled by this.  Do we believe it because we
> are shown proof, or because we want to believe it?

Actually, he's always had rather silly ideas.

(This is not a flame against Minsky, as I like a lot of the stuff he's
done. A major contributor and all. But who can doubt that he's come
out with some real corkers?)

The example of his silliness and his willingness to make wildly
implausible claims to the always-gullible press was this comment,
circa 1963, in "Life" magazine:

"In 20 years, computers will be smarter than us. The only question is
whether they'll keep us as pets."

Nothwithstanding the fact that I think my Macintosh has decided I am
its slave, put here to repair broken System files, to replace hard
disks, and to generally buy it new software, I think we see Minsky's
silliness at work. Even as a deliberately provocative, over-the-top
comment it was damaaging to AI in that it promulgated the view to many
that AI was mainly about building machines smarter than ourselves.

I met Minsky for the first time at a Hackers Conference and can only
say that in person he is so rude and dismissive of others as to make
anyone who's ever been on _this_ list look like Miss Manners.

The contrast with Richard Feynman, who was consistenly gracious and
rarely dismissive in a malicious sense, could not be greater. (Minsky
may not actually be malicious, he may just not be aware of how his
particular form of arrogance comes across. Who knows.)

In any case, Minsky's rudeness and silliness is just him being him.
Just as Rand's ideas were not necessarily invalidated by her 
personality, neither should Minsky's work be dimissed because of his often
silly predictions. There are better reasons to dismiss some of his work.

Personally, I think it's been many years since he was a major
contributor to the field.

--Tim May

